const passages = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"passageNumber\":1,\"title\":\"Ancient Agricultural Terracing in the High Andes\",\"subtitle\":\"Hydrological Engineering and Microclimatic Resilience of Pre-Columbian Farming\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nClinging to the razor-thin precipices of the high Andean cordillera, between three thousand and four thousand two hundred meters above sea level, lies one of the most sophisticated civil engineering feats of antiquity: the andenes terracing network. Constructed primarily between the twelfth and fifteenth centuries by pre-Inca cultures and later standardized during the zenith of the Inca Empire (Tawantinsuyu), these massive stone stepped stairways transformed vertical, barren mountain canyons into exceptionally fertile agro-ecological bastions capable of sustaining a population estimated at upwards of ten million souls.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nThe primary engineering challenge of high-altitude Andean agriculture was twofold: catastrophic diurnal temperature oscillations and severe slope erosion driven by seasonal torrential downpours. In the high altiplano, daytime solar radiation can elevate temperatures above twenty-five degrees Celsius, while clear night skies trigger severe sub-zero frosts capable of rupturing plant cellular membranes. To combat this freeze-thaw cycle, Inca master masons designed multi-layered thermal retention embankments. Behind exterior retaining walls made of porous volcanic basalt or fieldstone boulders, the Inca laid successive substrata of coarse river gravel, medium sand, and rich organic topsoil imported from lower subtropical valleys.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThis subterranean stratification served as an ingenious microclimatic heat battery. During daytime hours, the dark stone walls and coarse aggregate absorbed vast quantities of thermal energy. At night, as ambient temperatures plummeted, the basalt walls slowly re-radiated stored heat outward into the micro-basin, raising crop ambient temperatures by several critical degrees—frequently the narrow margin between vegetative survival and frost necrosis. Furthermore, the porous gravel matrix acted as an advanced sub-drainage system, rapidly percolating away violent cloudburst deluges that would otherwise trigger devastating mudslides, while preventing waterlogging around root networks.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nAt lower altitudes along the Lake Titicaca floodplains, pre-Columbian farmers engineered an equally brilliant complementary hydrological system known as waru waru, or raised-field agriculture. Waru waru consisted of elevated planting ridges alternating with deep perimeter canals filled with circulating water. The water stored in these trenches during sunny days released latent heat throughout the freezing nights, generating a thermal vapor blanket over the crop beds. Simultaneously, nutrient-dense organic silt and aquatic macrophyte algae accumulating at the bottom of the canals were periodically dredged and deposited onto the planting berms as nitrogen-rich bio-fertilizer.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nFollowing the Spanish conquest in the sixteenth century, the catastrophic demographic collapse of indigenous populations led to the widespread abandonment of up to seventy percent of these terrace networks. Spanish colonial authorities forcibly relocated surviving farming communities into valley-floor settlements to service mercury and silver mines, imposing European monoculture wheat and domestic cattle grazing. The hooves of heavy cattle compacted fragile mountain soils, and unmaintained drainage channels burst during rainy seasons, triggering systemic erosion that scarred thousands of hectares of ancestral mountain slopes.\\n\\nParagraph F\\nIn recent decades, however, contemporary agronomists and climate researchers have sparked an Andean terrace renaissance. With Andean glaciers retreating rapidly under global warming, highland farmers face unprecedented water scarcity during dry seasons and irregular torrential rain patterns. Non-governmental organizations, partnering with Quechua-speaking indigenous cooperatives, have painstakingly rehabilitated over fifty thousand hectares of ancient andenes. Recent hydrological field trials demonstrate that restored terraced hillsides retain sixty-five percent more soil moisture than non-terraced slopes, while reviving dozens of climate-resilient heirloom potato, quinoa, and maize landraces that conventional industrial farming had nearly erased.\",\"wordCount\":850,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1–6 on your answer sheet, write:\\nTRUE if the statement agrees with the information\\nFALSE if the statement contradicts the information\\nNOT GIVEN if there is no information on this\",\"questionType\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a6-q1\",\"questionNumber\":1,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"archaeology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Agricultural terracing in the Andes was initiated exclusively by the Inca civilization.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states the terraces were constructed primarily by 'pre-Inca cultures and later standardized during the zenith of the Inca Empire.'\",\"evidence\":\"Constructed primarily between the twelfth and fifteenth centuries by pre-Inca cultures and later standardized during the zenith of the Inca Empire\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q2\",\"questionNumber\":2,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"agriculture\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Night frosts in the high altiplano can cause structural damage to plant cells.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that sub-zero frosts are 'capable of rupturing plant cellular membranes.'\",\"evidence\":\"trigger severe sub-zero frosts capable of rupturing plant cellular membranes.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q3\",\"questionNumber\":3,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"engineering\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Inca masons used imported river gravel as the topmost layer of crop beds.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that the Inca laid successive substrata of coarse river gravel, medium sand, and rich organic topsoil on top.\",\"evidence\":\"laid successive substrata of coarse river gravel, medium sand, and rich organic topsoil imported from lower subtropical valleys.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q4\",\"questionNumber\":4,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"history\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Spanish colonial administrators actively encouraged the maintenance of high-altitude terrace irrigation canals.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E notes Spanish authorities relocated farming communities into valley floors, causing abandonment of terrace networks.\",\"evidence\":\"Spanish colonial authorities forcibly relocated surviving farming communities into valley-floor settlements to service mercury and silver mines\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q5\",\"questionNumber\":5,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"economics\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"Spanish mining operations in the Andes were more profitable than traditional farming.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E mentions relocating farmers to service mercury and silver mines, but makes no financial comparison of profitability between mining and traditional agriculture.\",\"evidence\":\"forcibly relocated surviving farming communities into valley-floor settlements to service mercury and silver mines\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q6\",\"questionNumber\":6,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"agriculture\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Restored terraces have been proven to hold significantly more soil moisture than un-terraced hillsides.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F cites field trials demonstrating that restored terraced hillsides retain sixty-five percent more soil moisture than non-terraced slopes.\",\"evidence\":\"Recent hydrological field trials demonstrate that restored terraced hillsides retain sixty-five percent more soil moisture than non-terraced slopes\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 7–13 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"sentence_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a6-q7\",\"questionNumber\":7,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"history\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"The andenes supported an Inca population estimated to exceed ____ million people.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"ten\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states the system sustained 'a population estimated at upwards of ten million souls.'\",\"evidence\":\"sustaining a population estimated at upwards of ten million souls.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q8\",\"questionNumber\":8,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"engineering\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Terrace exterior retaining walls were frequently constructed using porous volcanic ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"basalt\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B mentions exterior retaining walls made of 'porous volcanic basalt or fieldstone boulders'.\",\"evidence\":\"exterior retaining walls made of porous volcanic basalt or fieldstone boulders\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q9\",\"questionNumber\":9,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"agriculture\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"The lowermost gravel layer acted as an advanced sub-drainage system preventing ____ around plant roots.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"waterlogging\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes the gravel matrix prevented 'waterlogging around root networks.'\",\"evidence\":\"while preventing waterlogging around root networks.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q10\",\"questionNumber\":10,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"agriculture\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"The floodplain agricultural technique with elevated planting ridges was called ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"waru waru\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D introduces the system known as 'waru waru, or raised-field agriculture.'\",\"evidence\":\"complementary hydrological system known as waru waru, or raised-field agriculture.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q11\",\"questionNumber\":11,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"agriculture\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Canal trenches formed a protective thermal ____ over plants at night.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"vapor blanket\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D explains that water released latent heat, 'generating a thermal vapor blanket over the crop beds.'\",\"evidence\":\"generating a thermal vapor blanket over the crop beds.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q12\",\"questionNumber\":12,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"agriculture\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"Organic canal sediment was used as a natural, nitrogen-rich ____ on planting ridges.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"bio-fertilizer\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states sediment was deposited onto planting berms as 'nitrogen-rich bio-fertilizer.'\",\"evidence\":\"deposited onto the planting berms as nitrogen-rich bio-fertilizer.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q13\",\"questionNumber\":13,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"conservation\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Modern NGO partnerships have successfully rehabilitated more than ____ hectares of ancient terraces.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"fifty thousand\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F notes cooperatives have 'painstakingly rehabilitated over fifty thousand hectares of ancient andenes.'\",\"evidence\":\"painstakingly rehabilitated over fifty thousand hectares of ancient andenes.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":2,\"title\":\"The Re-emergence of Phage Therapy Against Antimicrobial Resistance\",\"subtitle\":\"Deploying Natural Viral Predators to Overcome Superbug Pathogens\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nThe discovery of penicillin by Alexander Fleming in 1928, followed by the mid-century golden age of antibiotic discovery, revolutionized modern medicine. Bacterial infections that had previously decimated entire communities became rapidly curable conditions. However, nearly a century of pervasive antibiotic overprescription in human healthcare, combined with the ubiquitous routine feeding of sub-therapeutic antibiotics to commercial livestock, has spawned a global public health catastrophe: antimicrobial resistance (AMR). The World Health Organization projects that by 2050, untreatable multidrug-resistant 'superbugs' could kill ten million people annually. In response to this looming pharmaceutical cliff, biomedical scientists are reviving a century-old biological therapeutic: bacteriophage therapy.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nBacteriophages—colloquially termed phages—are specialized viruses that infect and destroy bacteria. Discovered independently by British microbiologist Frederick Twort in 1915 and Franco-Canadian scientist Félix d'Hérelle in 1917, phages are the most abundant biological entities on the planet, numbering an estimated ten to the thirty-first power particles in the global biosphere. Unlike broad-spectrum chemical antibiotics, which obliterate pathogenic bacteria alongside beneficial human gut microbiota, bacteriophages are exquisitely species-specific. A particular phage will typically bind to and destroy only a single bacterial strain or species, leaving the host's wider commensal microbiome completely unharmed.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThe therapeutic mechanism relies on the lytic cycle. A virulent phage attaches via tail fibers to specific surface protein receptors on the targeted bacterial cell wall. Once docked, the phage acts like a microscopic hypodermic syringe, injecting its viral genetic payload (DNA or RNA) directly into the bacterial cytoplasm. The phage genes promptly hijack the host's cellular machinery, arresting bacterial replication and commanding the cell to synthesize hundreds of new phage particles. Finally, the viral genome produces two destructive enzymes: endolysin, which degrades the bacterial peptidoglycan cell wall from within, and holin, which perforates the inner membrane. The pressure builds until the bacterial cell bursts violently (lysis), releasing progeny phages into the surrounding tissue to attack neighboring bacteria.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nHistorically, Western clinical interest in phages waned in the 1940s due to the sudden industrial availability of stable, mass-produced chemical antibiotics. Furthermore, early phage preparations suffered from inconsistent purification, lack of molecular characterization, and premature clinical failures. In contrast, the Soviet Union—partially isolated from Western pharmaceutical markets—enthusiastically nurtured phage research. The Eliava Institute of Bacteriophage, Microbiology and Virology in Tbilisi, Georgia, continued treating human bacterial dysentery, battlefield wound sepsis, and chronic osteomyelitis with phage cocktails for decades, accumulating valuable clinical observations that Western institutions largely ignored.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nToday, a major advantage of phage therapy is its evolutionary dynamics. When bacteria develop resistance to chemical antibiotics through cellular mutations or plasmid-borne efflux pumps, the static antibiotic molecule cannot adapt. Conversely, phages are living biological systems capable of ongoing co-evolution. If a targeted bacterium alters its surface receptor to prevent phage attachment, natural selection favors mutant phages capable of recognizing the altered receptor. Furthermore, researchers frequently exploit an evolutionary trade-off known as 'phage steering': when bacteria mutate to evade phage predation by jettisoning surface efflux pumps or protective polysaccharide capsules, they frequently forfeit their resistance to standard antibiotics, restoring clinical susceptibility to drugs they had previously resisted.\\n\\nParagraph F\\nDespite promising results in compassionate-use clinical interventions, substantial logistical and regulatory hurdles remain. Because phages are customized biological organisms rather than standardized chemical molecules, traditional double-blind randomized clinical trial frameworks—designed for uniform chemical pills—fit phage regimens awkwardly. Phage cocktails often require dynamic reformulation to match an individual patient's evolving bacterial isolate. Regulatory agencies such as the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and European Medicines Agency (EMA) are actively formulating adaptive regulatory pathways to license bespoke phage bio-banks, heralding a new era of personalized biological medicine.\",\"wordCount\":880,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F, in boxes 14–20 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"matching_information\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a6-q14\",\"questionNumber\":14,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"medicine\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"A description of the step-by-step physical destruction of a bacterial cell.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C describes the lytic cycle: docking, genetic injection, hijacking, enzyme production, and cell lysis.\",\"evidence\":\"The phage genes promptly hijack the host's cellular machinery... producing two destructive enzymes: endolysin... and holin... until the bacterial cell bursts violently\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q15\",\"questionNumber\":15,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"history\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Mention of a research institute in Eastern Europe that preserved phage medicine during the Cold War.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D highlights the Eliava Institute in Tbilisi, Georgia, which continued phage treatments.\",\"evidence\":\"The Eliava Institute of Bacteriophage, Microbiology and Virology in Tbilisi, Georgia, continued treating human bacterial dysentery\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q16\",\"questionNumber\":16,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"The estimate of the total quantity of bacteriophage particles on Earth.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B gives the figure of an estimated ten to the thirty-first power particles in the global biosphere.\",\"evidence\":\"numbering an estimated ten to the thirty-first power particles in the global biosphere.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q17\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"medicine\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"A projection of global mortality rates caused by drug-resistant bacteria by mid-century.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A cites WHO projections of ten million deaths annually by 2050.\",\"evidence\":\"The World Health Organization projects that by 2050, untreatable multidrug-resistant 'superbugs' could kill ten million people annually.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q18\",\"questionNumber\":18,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"genetics\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"How attempting to resist phages can make bacteria vulnerable to antibiotics once again.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"E\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E describes 'phage steering', where bacteria mutating to evade phages lose antibiotic resistance.\",\"evidence\":\"when bacteria mutate to evade phage predation by jettisoning surface efflux pumps or protective polysaccharide capsules, they frequently forfeit their resistance to standard antibiotics\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q19\",\"questionNumber\":19,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"pharmacology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Regulatory difficulties in testing personalized phage mixtures with standard clinical trials.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"F\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F discusses how traditional double-blind randomized clinical trial frameworks fit phage regimens awkwardly.\",\"evidence\":\"traditional double-blind randomized clinical trial frameworks—designed for uniform chemical pills—fit phage regimens awkwardly.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q20\",\"questionNumber\":20,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"microbiology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Why bacteriophages do not damage the normal bacteria living in the human digestive system.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B emphasizes phage specificity: destroying only a single strain and leaving host microbiota unharmed.\",\"evidence\":\"bacteriophages are exquisitely species-specific. A particular phage will typically bind to and destroy only a single bacterial strain or species, leaving the host's wider commensal microbiome completely unharmed.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 21–26 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a6-q21\",\"questionNumber\":21,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"medicine\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Antimicrobial resistance has been exacerbated by the routine feeding of antibiotics to commercial ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"livestock\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A mentions routine feeding of antibiotics to 'commercial livestock'.\",\"evidence\":\"ubiquitous routine feeding of sub-therapeutic antibiotics to commercial livestock\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q22\",\"questionNumber\":22,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"microbiology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Phages begin an infection by binding to surface protein ____ on bacterial walls.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"receptors\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes attachment via tail fibers to 'specific surface protein receptors'.\",\"evidence\":\"attaches via tail fibers to specific surface protein receptors on the targeted bacterial cell wall.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q23\",\"questionNumber\":23,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"biochemistry\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"The enzyme ____ dissolves the bacterial cell wall from the inside.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"endolysin\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C identifies endolysin as degrading the peptidoglycan wall from within.\",\"evidence\":\"endolysin, which degrades the bacterial peptidoglycan cell wall from within\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q24\",\"questionNumber\":24,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"history\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Western medical interest in phages declined with the rise of mass-produced chemical ____ in the 1940s.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"antibiotics\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D refers to the sudden industrial availability of stable, mass-produced chemical antibiotics.\",\"evidence\":\"due to the sudden industrial availability of stable, mass-produced chemical antibiotics.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q25\",\"questionNumber\":25,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"evolution\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"The evolutionary trade-off where bacteria forfeit drug resistance is termed phage ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"steering\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E refers to the trade-off known as 'phage steering'.\",\"evidence\":\"exploit an evolutionary trade-off known as 'phage steering'\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q26\",\"questionNumber\":26,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"medicine\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Regulators are currently designing frameworks to license custom phage ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"bio-banks\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F mentions adaptive regulatory pathways to license bespoke phage bio-banks.\",\"evidence\":\"pathways to license bespoke phage bio-banks, heralding a new era of personalized biological medicine.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":3,\"title\":\"Algorithmic Fairness and Epistemic Bias in Automated Decision Systems\",\"subtitle\":\"The Societal Ramifications of Machine Learning Within Jurisprudence and Public Sector Governance\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nOver the past decade, administrative state bodies, commercial credit providers, and criminal judicial circuits have accelerated the deployment of automated decision systems (ADS). Powered by sophisticated machine learning architectures and statistical scoring matrices, these algorithmic instruments are marketed as objective, mathematical arbiters capable of expunging irrational human prejudice, inconsistency, and administrative fatigue from public governance. However, an expanding body of critical computer science scholarship reveals that far from guaranteeing pristine objectivity, algorithmic tools frequently automate, amplify, and mathematically legitimize historical human discrimination under an impenetrable facade of technological neutrality.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nA fundamental mechanism through which bias permeates predictive algorithms is the problem of training data contamination. Supervised machine learning models formulate predictive rules by discovering latent correlations within vast repositories of historical records. If the underlying historical records reflect systemic socioeconomic disparity or disproportionate law enforcement practices, the algorithm treats those historical inequities as ground-truth normative baselines. In criminal jurisprudence, risk assessment algorithms such as COMPAS (Correctional Offender Management Profiling for Alternative Sanctions) evaluate a defendant's statistical probability of recidivism. In 2016, an investigative audit demonstrated that the software exhibited substantial racial disparities: it generated twice as many false-positive predictions—mistakenly classifying non-reoffending individuals as high risk—for Black defendants compared to White defendants.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nEven when explicit demographic variables like race, gender, or religion are deliberately scrubbed from model inputs, algorithmic systems effortlessly circumvent these exclusions by leveraging proxy variables. In computer science, a proxy variable is an ostensibly neutral data point that correlates strongly with protected attributes. For example, commercial credit algorithms evaluating mortgage applicants frequently weigh postal codes, employment stability metrics, educational pedigree, and smartphone purchase logs. Because geographic housing patterns in many Western metropolises reflect decades of discriminatory zoning and redlining, an applicant's residential postal code operates as a mathematical surrogate for ethnic and socioeconomic heritage, effectively resurrecting banned biases through secondary correlations.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nCompounding this vulnerability is the mathematical incompatibility among differing definitions of fairness. In 2016, computer scientist Jon Kleinberg and his colleagues proved a landmark impossibility theorem regarding algorithmic equity: it is mathematically impossible for any risk prediction tool to concurrently satisfy three common, intuitive criteria of fairness—calibration (equal predictive probability across groups), error rate parity (equal false-positive and false-negative rates), and statistical independence—whenever the base rate of the predicted event differs between demographic groups. Consequently, system architects must deliberately choose which group fairness metric to prioritize, a decision that is fundamentally political, ethical, and ideological, rather than purely mathematical.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nFurthermore, the widespread adoption of proprietary 'black-box' deep learning models creates profound crises of due process and administrative transparency. Deep neural networks process information through millions of non-linear mathematical node weights, rendering their internal reasoning opaque even to the software engineers who designed them. When an algorithmic tool denies an individual bail, revokes unemployment benefits, or flags a parent for child welfare intervention, the affected citizen is denied meaningful epistemic access to the rationale behind the decision. Without the ability to interrogate the algorithmic logic, cross-examine data inputs, or identify erroneous records, the foundational democratic right to appeal administrative decisions is severely undermined.\\n\\nParagraph F\\nTo remediate these systemic hazards, leading legal scholars and computer scientists advocate for algorithmic impact assessments, mandatory external auditing, and the codification of a legally enforceable 'right to explanation' in administrative law. The European Union's Artificial Intelligence Act represents an initial legislative effort to categorize high-risk algorithmic applications, mandating continuous data governance, human-in-the-loop oversight, and rigorous adversarial bias testing prior to public deployment. Ultimately, society must recognize that mathematical models are not detached reflections of pure truth; they are human opinions encoded in mathematics, reflecting the values, blind spots, and power structures of their creators.\",\"wordCount\":880,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C, or D. Write the correct letter in boxes 27–32 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a6-q27\",\"questionNumber\":27,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"artificial intelligence\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Proponents of automated decision systems argue that algorithms can\",\"options\":[\"completely eliminate the need for human judges and civil servants.\",\"instantly detect fraudulent banking transactions with zero error.\",\"remove human bias, fatigue, and inconsistency from decisions.\",\"reduce government administrative budgets by half.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states algorithms are marketed as capable of expunging irrational human prejudice, inconsistency, and administrative fatigue.\",\"evidence\":\"marketed as objective, mathematical arbiters capable of expunging irrational human prejudice, inconsistency, and administrative fatigue from public governance.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q28\",\"questionNumber\":28,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"data science\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Machine learning algorithms incorporate historical bias primarily because\",\"options\":[\"software engineers deliberately insert malicious code.\",\"algorithms cannot process large quantities of data.\",\"hardware chips contain manufacturing flaws.\",\"training data derived from past records reflects existing inequalities.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that if underlying historical records reflect disparities, the algorithm treats those inequities as ground-truth baselines.\",\"evidence\":\"If the underlying historical records reflect systemic socioeconomic disparity or disproportionate law enforcement practices, the algorithm treats those historical inequities as ground-truth normative baselines.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q29\",\"questionNumber\":29,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"computer science\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"The audit of the COMPAS system in 2016 revealed that\",\"options\":[\"Black defendants were mistakenly categorized as high-risk at twice the rate of White defendants.\",\"White defendants were incorrectly scored as high-risk twice as often as Black defendants.\",\"the software had an overall accuracy rate of under thirty percent.\",\"the program failed to run on standard courthouse computers.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states it generated twice as many false-positive predictions for Black defendants compared to White defendants.\",\"evidence\":\"generated twice as many false-positive predictions—mistakenly classifying non-reoffending individuals as high risk—for Black defendants compared to White defendants.\"},{\"id\":\"a6-q30\",\"questionNumber\":30,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"computer science\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"According to Paragraph C, removing sensitive categories like race from an algorithm's inputs fails b